# Disorder in the Corporation
Tamil title: கார்ப்பரேஷன் அலங்கோலம்

The judgment delivered by the Tamil Nadu Congress Working Committee, based upon the report of the local-bodies inquiry committee that investigated corruption in the Madras Corporation, has now been published. Six councillors—Comrades Vinayaka Mudaliar, Damodaram Naidu, Gopala Ratnam, Adikesavalu Naicker, D. G. Balasubramaniam, and Lakshmanasami Mudaliar—have been punished for corruption in the Corporation.

Among these six, Comrades Gopala Ratnam, Adikesavalu Naicker, and D. G. Lakshmanasami Mudaliar are old Congressmen and patriots who went to prison. The other three are new devotees of Congress. Of them, only Comrade Damodaram Naidu had long been a member of the Justice Party before entering Congress. We need hardly explain how mischievous *Dinamani* is in claiming that corruption spread through the Corporation because people from the Justice Party entered Congress.

We shall now set out the offences attributed to those punished. Comrade Vinayaka Mudaliar is said to have used his financial influence to gather several Congress councillors around him, both to build his own influence and to ensure that the Corporation administration proceeded according to his wishes. The faction he assembled reportedly met at his house before meetings of the Congress party, decided how matters coming before the party should be resolved, and generally carried those decisions through at the party meeting.

Besides acting in this manner within the party, members of the faction reportedly held majorities on some important standing committees of the Corporation and conducted business as they pleased.

Comrade Gopala Ratnam, a member of the Madras District Congress Committee, is said to have supported this faction and, on many occasions, used its influence for himself and some within it. He had reportedly behaved this way from the election onward. Comrade Damodaram Naidu is said to have misused his influence. Comrade Balasubramaniam too is said to have conducted affairs with self-interest in mind.

The offences committed by Comrades D. G. Lakshmanasami Mudaliar and Adikesavalu Naicker have not been clearly published. Nevertheless, they are required to resign their standing-committee posts and may not serve on standing committees for as long as they remain Corporation members.

The Tamil Nadu Congress Working Committee has therefore ruled that Vinayaka Mudaliar, Damodaram Naidu, Gopala Ratnam, and Balasubramaniam must resign as members of the Corporation. Lakshmanasami and Adikesavalu Naicker have been permitted only to remain Corporation members.

It does not appear, however, that those punished were given an opportunity to present their respective cases. Whether this judgment is legally valid therefore deserves consideration. Moreover, the Tamil Nadu Congress Working Committee that pronounced it has no authority to enforce it. If those punished do not accept the ruling, no one can compel them. We therefore have nothing further to say until their course of action becomes known.

The Tamil Nadu Congress Working Committee has also expressed a view on the press agitation. It says that nationally minded newspapers should not agitate publicly when they find faults in Congress organizations or related institutions. Considering the need to reform those bodies, they should instead write privately to the institutions about the defects or corruption and attempt to make them honest. Public agitation in newspapers, it says, supplies hostile newspapers and opposition parties with material for attacking Congress and thereby harms the Congress organization. The committee regrets that certain nationalist newspapers caused such injury.

Regarding the controversy over Comrade Bhaktavatsalam’s resignation, the committee merely observed that it did not appear proper for him, on seeing extensive corruption in the Congress party, to send his resignation directly to the Corporation and consent to have his view of the resignation published in the newspapers. It said that his statement, personal testimony, and other evidence did not establish that his conduct was right, and that anyone elected in the name of Congress should always send a resignation to the head of the relevant organization.

The committee that imposed strict punishment upon Corporation members has let off without consequence both the nationalist newspapers that acted contrary to party policy and brought disgrace upon Congress, and Comrade Bhaktavatsalam. This naturally raises suspicion.

At a farewell dinner given by Comrade Sathyamurthi to the departing mayor, Comrade Sriramulu Naidu, the Honourable Local Administration Minister Gopal Reddy said that unless corruption in the Corporation ended, he would dissolve the Corporation itself. Sathyamurthi replied that corruption was not as extensive as many imagined and reportedly gave the minister a “dose,” saying it was wrong to frighten people with the spectre of dissolving the Corporation.

*Dinamani* did not like this. Condemning Sathyamurthi, it wrote that he had said some angry people were exaggerating the matter and that the Corporation was not so bad. He had even dared advise the minister that it was inappropriate to speak now of dissolving the Corporation. *Dinamani* said it did not believe anything had been exaggerated: only one part in a thousand had emerged before the inquiry committee, so there was no sense in Sathyamurthi becoming angry over exaggeration or setting out to advise the minister.

In the present circumstances, we do not wish to pronounce a final view. *Dinamani* says only one-thousandth of the Corporation’s corruption has come before the inquiry committee. We shall therefore say nothing until the remaining 999 parts emerge. They will certainly emerge very soon. Letters about corruption in the Corporation arrive and accumulate here every day. We are examining them and arranging to publish them with adequate evidence. It is also important to learn the views of those who have been punished.

Let the people of the country decide how much glory it brings Congress that the Corporation captured by Congressmen has reached the point where the question is whether it should be dissolved.

---NOTES---
No publication date survives in this canonical OCR file. Administrative expressions such as standing committee, local-bodies inquiry, and Working Committee are rendered by function. The source repeats part of the Working Committee passage and contains several name and numeral OCR errors, all logged separately.
